  "startedAt": "2026-11-05T14:01:20.857420+00:00",
  "raw": "{\n  \"audits\": [\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q001\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_154\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"A: -x(x-1)^2\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Factoring gives -x^3+2x^2-x=-x(x^2-2x+1)=-x(x-1)^2. This combines extracting a common factor with factoring a perfect-square trinomial, matching cnm_154. Choice D is equivalent to the original expression but is not completely factored.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q002\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_157\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"D\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Since x-y=-(y-x), (x-y)/(y-x)=-1 whenever x≠y. A and B are not general identities, even where their denominators are nonzero. C additionally requires the multiplier m to be nonzero at the values considered; allowing any polynomial expression permits a zero multiplier and an undefined denominator. The nonzero-multiplier restriction and sign transformation directly match cnm_157.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q003\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p132\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_p125\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"31.4\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Direct calculation gives 3.14×7=21.98 and 3.14×3=9.42, followed by 21.98+9.42=31.4. This is a defensible route assessing decimal-by-integer multiplication with decimal addition as support. Alternatively, distributivity gives 3.14(7+3)=3.14×10=31.4. The candidate's direct-calculation sequence is therefore a valid selected method, not a universally required sequence; the alternative does not require expanding the accepted scope to their union.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q004\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_152\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"A: (x^2+4)(x+2)(x-2)\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Applying the difference-of-squares identity twice gives x^4-16=(x^2+4)(x^2-4)=(x^2+4)(x+2)(x-2). Under the usual real-coefficient interpretation, x^2+4 has no further nonconstant real factors. Choice B stops before factoring x^2-4. Repeated application and complete factorization are both covered by cnm_152.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q005\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_150\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"C\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"The identity rewrites the polynomial x^2-1 as the product of the polynomial expressions x+1 and x-1. Read from left to right, this is factorization; expanding the right-hand side would be the inverse operation. The task directly assesses the definition in cnm_150.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q006\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_150\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"2\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Only transformations (2) and (4) rewrite a polynomial as a product of polynomial expressions. Transformation (1) expands a product, while (3) ends in the sum a(a+2)+1 rather than a product representing the entire polynomial. Thus there are two factorizations, and classification by cnm_150 is the appropriate assessed scope.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}",
